 * Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: `$alternative = Alternative::TwoSided`.
 *
 * @param list<float> $sample At least two finite observations.
 * @param callable(float): float $cdf Nonempty continuous-null CDF callable, returning values in [0,1].
 * @param Alternative|int $alternative TwoSided (default), Less if the empirical CDF is below the reference, or Greater if above.
 * @return HypothesisTestResult KS discrepancy, sample size in parameter1, and approximate p-value. Exceptions from the CDF propagate.
 * @throws InvalidArgumentException C++ `std::invalid_argument`: If inputs violate the stated constraints.
 * @throws NativeException C++ `std::bad_alloc`: If allocation fails.
 * @see ks_test_one_sample_normal()
 * @see ks_test_two_sample()
 */
function ks_test_one_sample(array $sample, callable $cdf, Alternative|int $alternative = Alternative::TwoSided): HypothesisTestResult
{
    return \Datamunge\Rosetta\Runtime::call(209, [...\array_slice([$sample, \Datamunge\Rosetta\Runtime::callable($cdf), $alternative], 0, \func_num_args())]);
}

/**
 * One-sample KS test against a fully specified continuous normal CDF.
 *
 * Calls C++ `datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal`.
 *
 * Two-sided p-values use the Stephens corrected asymptotic Kolmogorov reference; one-sided
 * values use exp(-2*n*D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed independently of this sample: fitting them
 * here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a Lilliefors test. Ties are accepted, but
 * continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage, input
 * unchanged, no omission/shared state.
 *
 * **Example**
 *
 * ```
 * const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
 * std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3
 * ```
 *
 * **References**
 *
 * - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
 *
 * Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: `$mean = 0.0`, `$sd = 1.0`, `$alternative = Alternative::TwoSided`.
 *
 * @param list<float> $x At least two finite observations.
 * @param float $mean Finite prespecified normal mean, default zero.
 * @param float $sd Positive finite prespecified normal SD, default one.
 * @param Alternative|int $alternative TwoSided (default); Greater tests empirical CDF above reference, Less below (opposite to a location alternative).
 * @return HypothesisTestResult statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n and approximate p_value; no CI.
 * @throws InvalidArgumentException C++ `std::invalid_argument`: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mean/sd or alternative.
 * @see HypothesisTestResult
 */
function ks_test_one_sample_normal(array $x, float $mean = 0.0, float $sd = 1.0, Alternative|int $alternative = Alternative::TwoSided): HypothesisTestResult
{
    return \Datamunge\Rosetta\Runtime::call(210, [...\array_slice([$x, $mean, $sd, $alternative], 0, \func_num_args())]);
}

/**
 * One-sample KS test against a specified continuous uniform CDF.
