 *
 * @param list<float> $x At least two finite observations.
 * @param float $lower Finite prespecified lower bound, default zero.
 * @param float $upper Finite upper bound greater than lower, default one.
 * @param Alternative|int $alternative TwoSided (default), Greater for empirical CDF above reference or Less below.
 * @return HypothesisTestResult statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n, approximate p_value; no CI.
 * @throws InvalidArgumentException C++ `std::invalid_argument`: Invalid sample, limits or alternative.
 * @see HypothesisTestResult
 */
function ks_test_one_sample_uniform(array $x, float $lower = 0.0, float $upper = 1.0, Alternative|int $alternative = Alternative::TwoSided): HypothesisTestResult
{
    return \Datamunge\Rosetta\Runtime::call(211, [...\array_slice([$x, $lower, $upper, $alternative], 0, \func_num_args())]);
}

/**
 * Two-sample KS comparison of empirical CDFs with asymptotic p-values.
 *
 * Calls C++ `datamunge::stats::ks_test_two_sample`.
 *
 * Uses effective n=n_x*n_y/(n_x+n_y) in the asymptotic reference. This is not an exact finite-
 * sample calculation; ties/rounding and very small groups affect calibration. O(N log N)
 * work/O(N) storage; inputs unchanged, no omission/shared state.
